extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Features | Annotations"
	- pagedescription = "Annotations in Tablechart: brackets, trend arrows and reference lines, placed by the data."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Annotations
				p.lead A bracket, a trend arrow or a reference line, placed by the data.

	+section("How to add an annotation")
		p Put a #[code div] with the class #[code chart-annotations] inside the chart. Each annotation in it is a #[code span] with #[code data-annotation]. An annotation is placed by the data, so if you change a number, the annotation moves with it.

		+codeblock('HTML')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				table …
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="bracket") +11%

	+section("Bracket")
		p A bracket shows the change between two bars. If the chart has two bars, no other attributes are needed.

		+chart(null, '9')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Apple juice
					br
					span thousands of litres
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Last season', '251.5'], ['This season', '279.6']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="bracket") +11%

	+section("Choose the bars")
		p If the chart has more bars, set #[code data-from] and #[code data-to] to the row numbers. The first row is #[code 1].

		+chart(null, '11')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['2021', '74'], ['2022', '80'], ['2023', '91'], ['2024', '102'], ['2025', '116']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="bracket" data-from="2" data-to="5") +45%

	+section("Trend")
		p A trend arrow runs from the first value to the last. #[code data-at] sets where the badge is along the arrow, from #[code 0] to #[code 1].
		p Set #[code data-years] to the number of years. Tablechart calculates the yearly rate, and shows a warning in the console if it is different from yours. Your number is always the one shown.
		p A #[code span] with the class #[code tablechart-caption] is shown above the badge.

		+chart(null, '12')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Apples per tree
					br
					span each
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['2021', '74'], ['2022', '80'], ['2023', '91'], ['2024', '102'], ['2025', '116']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="trend" data-years="4" data-at="0.46")
						span.tablechart-caption A year
						span +12%

	+section("Reference line")
		p A reference line marks a value, such as a target or the mean. To put it on the value axis, add #[code data-value]. The line then goes across a column, line or area chart, and up a bar chart. #[code data-value] is a number, #[code mean] or #[code median]. The mean and the median are of the values in the table.

		+chart(null, '11')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption: b Apples picked
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '41']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="reference" data-value="60") Target

		p If the names of the rows are numbers, add #[code data-name] to put the line among them, as if the names were a scale. Here each row is a number of apples on a branch, and its value is how many branches have that many. If you use #[code mean] or #[code median], each name counts as often as its value: the mean is the mean number of apples on a branch.

		+chart(null, '13')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption: b Branches by number of apples
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['2', '3'], ['3', '8'], ['4', '12'], ['5', '9'], ['6', '5'], ['7', '2']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="reference" data-name="mean") Mean 4.3
					span(data-annotation="reference" data-name="median" data-line="dotted") Median 4

		p The line is dashed. If you add #[code data-line="dotted"], it is dotted. The text of the annotation is the label, on the line. If two labels would cover each other, the second one moves beside its line, or one line lower. If the annotation has no text, there is only the line.
		p If the label has a number and the line uses #[code mean] or #[code median], Tablechart checks it, to the decimals you wrote, and shows a warning in the console if they differ. Your text is always the one shown.

	+section("Break the scale")
		p A break in the scale is not an annotation, but it is added the same way. See #[a(href=url('/features/scale-break.html')) Scale break].

	+section("Annotations on several lines")
		p On a chart with several lines, #[code data-series] sets which line an annotation uses. The first line is #[code 1], and is used if #[code data-series] is not set.

		+codeblock('HTML')
			span(data-annotation="trend" data-series="2" data-years="9") +8%
